  "caveats": [
    "THE BOOTH INTERIOR IS ABOUT FOUR STOPS UNDER-LIT AT THE SPECCED LUMENS, and that is a fault in the spec's booth budget, not in the placement. The booth-interior light was raycast in place: 3.25 in of clear air above it to the ceiling deck at z 81.56 and 11.12 in below - it is inside the booth, not trapped in the roof tray. The two interior frames metered mean luminance 0.0080 and 0.0097 against image-qa's 0.12 floor. A measured ladder arm at x16 on the booth-budget roles ONLY (booth interior 1780 -> 28482 lm, foam graze 890 -> 14241 lm) brings them to 0.0969 and 0.0918 and the pictures become properly readable - both files are kept as *-boothx16.png. The mechanism: BOOTH_FC 30 fc / CU 0.6 assumes ordinary room reflectances, and a WhisperRoom interior is charcoal panels plus dark blue acoustic foam whose material is a flat-diffuse shim with no reflection layer at all (observed, prior lane). Almost nothing comes back. The fix is one constant and it is BENTON'S CALL, not mine - so the shipped table is unchanged.",
    "RENDER TIME. A 1600x900 Medium frame of THIS scene does not converge in seven minutes; the first one hit a 420 s ceiling still rendering. The 53.1 s figure from the look sweep was itself taken under a 1-minute progressive_maxTime budget on a much lighter scene. The six delivered frames were rendered under a HARNESS budget of progressive_maxTime = 2.5 MINUTES (the parameter is in minutes, not seconds - observed: 150 was accepted and stopped nothing), which is the same mechanism lookdev-matrix.rb uses. Benton's 0.0 was restored afterwards and read back as 0.0. Everything else - 1600x900, progressive_maxSubdivs 20, threshold 0.04 - was never written. The frames are visibly noisy as a result.",
    "ONE FRAME PER BRIDGE JOB, and it is not a style choice. Six frames issued from a single Ruby job came back BYTE-IDENTICAL: frame 1 'ended' after 156 s and frames 2..6 all read 'never_started'. V-Ray will not begin a second production render inside one Ruby job, and save_vfb_image then writes the first frame's pixels again with nothing saying a render did not happen. The delivered run is six separate bridge jobs and the driver checks that the six images are six distinct files - they are.",
    "THE SUN IS ON, at V-Ray's default. /SunLight is on the NEVER_WRITE list, so the tool did not touch it and neither did the harness. The room is capped with four walls, so the only opening is the 36 in door; the sun is dressing, not a source, but it is not zero and the spec's lumens were derived for a sun-off room.",
    "/Standard Light exists in this scene, enabled, at intensity 2500, as it does in every new V-Ray model. It was not written and it is not part of the rig. It is recorded in vray_settings_at_render so nobody has to wonder.",
    "THE PENDANT AND A CEILING DRUM LAND 28 IN APART. \u00a76 sends the ceiling ambient to the grid points FURTHEST from the booth and the pendant to the floor-polygon corner FURTHEST from the booth, so in a room with one booth they converge on the same corner. Visible in rig-5-fittings, where the two fixtures sit side by side. A future rule should keep the pendant clear of the chosen ceiling points; it is a placement question, not a lumen one.",
    "BOTH CEILING DRUMS LANDED ON THE SAME SIDE OF THE ROOM. The 80 in booth inflated by the 12 in keep-out pad kills the whole middle column of a 240 x 192 Soft grid, leaving only x = 40, so 'the two furthest from the booth' had two candidates and took both. The east half of the room has no ceiling ambient over it; the key and rim carry the booth instead.",
    "F2's SHADE RENDERS OPAQUE, exactly as \u00a77.3 predicted. Light escapes top and bottom, not through the shade wall. Fixing it needs a translucent V-Ray material and this tool may not create materials - writing a newly created material plugin hangs SketchUp (observed, four force-kills, prior lane).",
    "THE FIXTURES ARE DRAWN AT 16 SEGMENTS PER CIRCLE, not the spec's 24. At 24 the five fixtures cost about 590 faces against a 600 budget with no headroom; at 16 they cost 273. SEG is one constant.",
    "The six saved scenes (rig-1..rig-6) are the HARNESS's, not the tool's, and they were left in the scratch model. The tool's own contribution to a model that has been swept is the WR_DropLights dictionary entry recording the exposure stamp, and the /CameraPhysical ISO itself - both by design, both documented, both revertible in one number.",
    "The rig read back in this file is a re-press made AFTER the six frames, because the restore test swept the rendered rig. It is the same code with the same inputs and every lumen, Kelvin, size and world position is identical by construction; only the V-Ray plugin NAMES differ from the ones the frames were rendered with."
  ],
  "images_note": "Every frame is kept twice: the full 1600x900 render, and a modest 1024x576 copy under web/ for embedding as base64. The eight web copies total about 3.9 MB (~5.2 MB base64); the full renders total 19 MB.",
